            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Let ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "f(x)=\\sqrt{x-x^2}",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ".",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p49-q47-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the value(s) of $x$ in the domain of $f$ where $f'(x)$ is undefined.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=0$ and $x=1$.",
          "worked": "The domain is $0\\le x\\le1$, since $x-x^2=x(1-x)\\ge0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f'(x)&=\\frac12(x-x^2)^{-1/2}(1-2x)\\\\f'(x)&=\\frac{1-2x}{2\\sqrt{x-x^2}},\\qquad 0<x<1.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe denominator is zero at $x=0$ and $x=1$. Both are in the domain, so $f'(x)$ is undefined at these values.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 46
      },
      {
        "id": "p49-q47-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Explain geometrically why the curve is defined at both points found in part (a), but the derivative is not.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The graph is the upper semicircle centred at $\\left(\\frac12,0\\right)$ with radius $\\frac12$. Its endpoints at $x=0$ and $x=1$ have vertical tangents, whose gradients are undefined.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y^2&=x-x^2\\\\y^2+\\left(x-\\frac12\\right)^2&=\\frac14.\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $y=\\sqrt{x-x^2}\\ge0$, the graph is the upper semicircle centred at $\\left(\\frac12,0\\right)$ with radius $\\frac12$. The endpoints $(0,0)$ and $(1,0)$ lie on the curve, so the curve is defined there. Its tangents at both endpoints are vertical, so their gradients, and hence $f'(0)$ and $f'(1)$, are undefined.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 56
      }
    ]
  },
